---
import MainLayout from '../../../../src/layouts/MainLayout/MainLayout.astro';
import DocsSidebar from '../../../../src/components/sidebar/DocsSidebar.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, SIDEBAR } from '../../site-config';

const slug = 'components/docs-toc';

const headings = [
    { depth: 2, slug: 'introduction', text: 'Introduction' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'props', text: 'Props' },
    { depth: 3, slug: 'headings-prop', text: 'headings prop' },
    { depth: 3, slug: 'toc-config-prop', text: 'tocConfig prop' },
    { depth: 3, slug: 'label-prop', text: 'label prop' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'behaviour', text: 'Behaviour' },
    { depth: 2, slug: 'styling', text: 'Styling' },
];
---

<MainLayout title="DocsToc" siteTitle={SITE_TITLE} hasSidebar currentSlug={slug} headings={headings}>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" items={SIDEBAR} currentSlug={slug} />

  <main class="content">
    <h1>DocsToc</h1>
    <p>
      <code>DocsToc</code> renders a "On this page" table of contents from an
      array of <code>MarkdownHeading</code> objects. In a real docs page these
      come from Astro's <code>getHeadings()</code> helper; here a static list
      is used so you can see the component in action without an MDX file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scroll the page — the active link is highlighted as each section crosses
      the 160 px threshold from the top of the viewport.
    </p>

    <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
    <p>
      The component is self-contained: it ships its own scroll-spy logic as a
      custom element (<code>&lt;docs-toc&gt;</code>) and all styles as a global
      stylesheet so the <code>.toc-sidebar</code> host element receives the
      correct layout rules automatically.
    </p>

    <h2 id="props">Props</h2>
    <p>Three props control the output at the root call site.</p>

    <h3 id="headings-prop">headings prop</h3>
    <p>
      An array of <code>MarkdownHeading</code> objects (<code>depth</code>,
      <code>slug</code>, <code>text</code>). Pass the value returned by
      <code>getHeadings()</code> directly from your MDX layout.
    </p>

    <h3 id="toc-config-prop">tocConfig prop</h3>
    <p>
      Controls which heading levels are included. Accepts <code>false</code>
      (renders nothing), <code>undefined</code> (default: h2–h3), or an object
      with optional <code>minHeadingLevel</code> / <code>maxHeadingLevel</code>
      fields.
    </p>

    <h3 id="label-prop">label prop</h3>
    <p>
      The accessible heading text rendered above the list. Defaults to
      <em>"On this page"</em>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="behaviour">Behaviour</h2>
    <p>
      The custom element uses <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>-throttled
      scroll listeners and <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> to determine
      which heading is currently active, then sets <code>aria-current="true"</code>
      on the matching link.
    </p>

    <h2 id="styling">Styling</h2>
    <p>
      The component injects global styles for the <code>.toc-sidebar</code>
      host, the <code>docs-toc</code> custom element, and the
      <code>.toc-list</code> tree. On viewports narrower than 84 rem the
      sidebar is hidden entirely via <code>display: none</code>.
    </p>
  </main>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .content {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem 6rem;
  }
  .content h1 { margin-top: 0; }
  .content code {
    background: var(--sl-color-bg-inline-code);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 0.25em;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
</style>
